course: dictionary
lesson: 126
title: Advantage
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: An advantage is something that helps you do better or makes you more likely to win. «Taking turns going first is fair when going first gives an advantage.» Going first helps you, so it is an advantage.
explain: The opposite of an advantage is a disadvantage. In school you might list the advantages and disadvantages of living in a city, or of using plastic.
explain: Some phrases use advantage too. You can have an advantage over someone, or do something to your advantage, which means in a way that helps you. Be careful: «Cheating is breaking the rules in secret to gain an unfair advantage.» Cheating is not fair to the others.
fact: An advantage is something that helps you do better or succeed.
fact: A disadvantage is the opposite: something that makes things harder for you.
fact: «Cheating is breaking the rules in secret to gain an unfair advantage.»
fact: In tennis, advantage is the point won right after deuce.
fact: Phrases: take advantage of (use a chance well, or unfairly use someone); have an advantage over someone; to your advantage.
exercise: In a game, a team with more players has what? | a bad start | *an advantage | a rule about turns | a shorter game
exercise: What is the opposite of an advantage? | a good point | a tennis point | an advantage that helps | *a disadvantage
exercise: «Cheating is breaking the rules in secret to gain an unfair advantage.» What does unfair advantage mean here? | a long break | *something that helps you win, but is not fair | a kind of fair game | a team shirt
test: In tennis, what is advantage? | the score when the game starts | the net | *the point won right after deuce | a rule about serving
test: What does «to your advantage» mean? | in a way that hurts you | in secret | very slowly | *in a way that helps you
exam: Ella says «take advantage of» always means being kind. Is she right? | *No, it can mean use a chance well, or unfairly use someone | Yes, it means to hurt someone | No, it means to lose a point | Yes, it always means being kind
